Chapter: RiscVStdio
  cites Foreword chapter StringUtils

Section: Body

 The wasm transport half for riscv, the sibling of `RiscVPlug`'s serial one.
 `RiscVPlug` writes the wire a byte at a time through `port-out-byte`, which
 is x86 port I/O and does not exist on this target -- a wasm build of that
 chapter refuses with `[UNSUPPORTED] port-in-byte`. `rv-build-wire-output`
 assembles the identical wire as a byte list, and `write-binary` hands it
 over whole.

 `rv-build-wire-output` had NO callers before this chapter (its signature and
 its definition were its only two mentions in the tree), so it is graded
 against the serial path rather than trusted: the wire this emits must be
 byte-identical to the one bare metal sends for the same IR.

 TWO OUTPUTS, chosen by a first line, because the wire is a payload and a
 board wants a FILE. Default is the wire, unchanged and still what the
 identity arm grades. `ELF` answers a RISC-V ELF64 built by `rv-build-elf`,
 this plug's own writer, which needs no other plug in the chain.

 `rv-build-elf` had no callers either -- the same L-UNCALLED shape as the wire
 builder, and for the same reason: it was written for a caller that did not
 exist yet. This is it.

 THE ENTRY IS `__start`, THE RUNTIME INIT, NOT `opening`. `__start` is what
 establishes the stack pointer and the heap pointer in S1 that every emitted
 function then assumes; entering at `opening` runs the program with neither,
 which is the same mistake `cdx-to-pe.ps1` names on the x86 side where its
 `-EntryStart` switch exists precisely to avoid it. The offset is looked up
 rather than assumed to be zero, because emitted functions are not laid down
 in source order and offset zero is whichever one happened to be first. A unit
 with no `__start` is REFUSED by name.

  plug-emit-ir-bytes : Text -> [Console] Nothing
  plug-emit-ir-bytes (input) =
   let want-elf = text-starts-with input "ELF\n"
   in let ir-text = if want-elf then text-drop input 4 else input
   in let parsed = parse-ir-chapter ir-text
   in let cg-state = rv-emit-module empty-rv-state (parsed.chapter) (parsed.type-defs) (parsed.effect-op-names)
   in if want-elf then rv-emit-board-elf cg-state
   else write-binary (rv-build-wire-output cg-state)

 `rv-record-func` stores `st.insn-count`, an INSTRUCTION INDEX, not a byte
 offset -- `rv-patch-calls-loop` multiplies by 4 to reach bytes and so does
 the auipc fixup. `rv-build-elf` adds its argument to the load address as
 BYTES, so the index has to be scaled here. Unscaled it produced an entry a
 quarter of the way to the right instruction and, worse, an ODD address:
 0x80002D83 for factorial, which a RISC-V core rejects outright since even
 compressed instructions are 2-byte aligned. QEMU showed it as a silent hang
 with no output, which is what any bad entry looks like from outside.

  rv-emit-board-elf : RvState -> [Console] Nothing
  rv-emit-board-elf (st) =
   let idx = rv-find-func-offset (st.func-names) (st.func-offsets) "__start" 0
   in if idx < 0 then print-line-uni "REFUSED this unit has no `__start`, so a board kernel would have no runtime init to enter"
   else write-binary (rv-build-elf (__buf-read-bytes (st.code-buf) 0 (st.code-pos)) (st.data) (idx * 4))
